---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/expert/quantum-field-theory">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the deepest stop on the physics track, and it needs exactly two advanced lessons
    as its floor: <em>Quantum foundations</em> (states, superposition, the uncertainty
    budget) and <em>Special relativity</em> (rest energy, the speed limit, spacetime). The
    classical field picture from <em>Electrostatics</em> and <em>Magnetism and induction</em>
    is the thing about to be quantised — and the energy bookkeeping the whole track has
    practised is what survives every reaction below. QFT merges the two advanced toolkits —
    nothing else in the atlas asks you to hold both at once.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the field ontology: fields are fundamental, particles are their quanta, and all electrons are identical for that reason;</li>
    <li>map alpha, beta and gamma emissions onto the strong, weak and electromagnetic forces;</li>
    <li>use the 1.022 MeV threshold of pair production and connect the annihilation loop to PET scanning;</li>
    <li>explain why exponential decay is a theorem of quantum theory, not a modelling convenience;</li>
    <li>summarise what the Standard Model accounts for — and its three known gaps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Quantum field theory (QFT) merges quantum mechanics with special relativity — and in
    doing so it <strong>demotes particles from protagonists to bit-players</strong>. The
    fundamental objects are fields filling all space; particles are their quantised ripples.
    Two electrons anywhere in the universe are identical because they are the <em>same</em>
    field, plucked twice. The nuclear physics syllabus — the nuclear atom, radioactivity,
    half-life, detection, safety — is the historical on-ramp to this theory: every one of
    its topics was a doorway QFT later walked through, from Rutherford's nucleus (which
    forced the question "what holds it together?") to beta decay (whose missing energy
    predicted the neutrino) to half-life (which is quantum randomness, exactly).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the conceptual ladder of QFT, from fields to the Standard Model" widget="equationStep" config={smCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> press "Next step" up the five-rung ladder. Step 1 sets
    the ontology — fields everywhere, one per particle type; step 2 explains particle
    identity as literal sameness of field; step 3 recasts forces as exchanges; step 4
    introduces the virtual-particle bookkeeping, with the honest caveat that the picture is
    a popularisation while the measurements are exact; step 5 is the inventory. Watch each
    rung demote the particle a little further.
  </p>

  <h2>The nuclear atom: scales and the discovery that started it</h2>
  <p>
    The atom (5.1.1) measures about 10⁻¹⁰ m across; the nucleus (5.1.2) is about 10⁻¹⁵ m —
    100,000 times smaller, with over 99.9% of the mass. That emptiness was discovered by
    firing alpha particles at gold foil (Geiger and Marsden, 1909, under Rutherford): most
    sailed through, a tiny fraction bounced back — as Rutherford put it, as shocking as a
    15-inch shell bouncing off tissue paper. The electron, found by Thomson (1897), weighs
    1/1,836 of a proton, so the nucleus carries essentially all the mass in 10⁻¹⁴ of the
    volume. The immediate question — protons are positive and packed together, so
    electrostatic repulsion should explode the nucleus instantly — has an answer that
    foreshadows the whole Standard Model: the <strong>strong nuclear force</strong>, an
    attraction between nucleons about 100 times stronger than electromagnetism but effective
    only across a couple of femtometres. Its range is the nucleus's size; its quantum
    description (quantum chromodynamics, quarks exchanging gluons) is a pillar of QFT. The
    neutron (Chadwick, 1932) completed the picture: Z protons set the element, A nucleons
    set the mass, and the neutron's presence buys binding without charge.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="guess the magnitude — how empty is the atom?" widget="estimate" config={qftEmptyEst} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "atoms are mostly empty space, so matter is mostly
    nothing." The space in an atom is filled with the electron's wavefunction and the
    electromagnetic field it sustains — and it is exactly this "filled emptiness" that
    resists compression: your hand stops at a table because the electrons in your skin and
    the table's surface cannot occupy the same quantum states (the Pauli exclusion
    principle) and their fields repel before nuclei ever touch. Matter is not mostly
    nothing; it is mostly field, and the fields are what you have been touching all along.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="true or false — what the 'empty' space actually is" widget="judge" config={qftEmptyJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the atom's emptiness posed the question — what holds the
    nucleus together — and the answer, a force with femtometre range, already pointed past
    classical physics. Next, the three kinds of radioactivity turn out to be a map of three
    fundamental forces.
  </p>

  <h2>Radioactivity: three emissions, three forces</h2>
  <p>
    Radioactivity (5.2.2, 5.2.3) sorts into three emissions, and the sorting is a map of
    three fundamental forces. <strong>Alpha</strong>: a helium nucleus (2 protons, 2
    neutrons), a few MeV, stopped by paper or a few centimetres of air — the strong force
    losing a fragment. <strong>Beta</strong>: an electron (or positron) from the nucleus at
    a few MeV, penetrating aluminium-millimetre territory — the weak force converting a
    neutron into a proton, electron and antineutrino. <strong>Gamma</strong>: a high-energy
    photon, no mass, penetrating centimetres of lead — the electromagnetic force shedding
    excess energy after a decay. That gamma rays are photons (quanta of Maxwell's field) is
    the first QFT fact in the syllabus; that beta decay produces a neutrino was predicted
    by Pauli (1930) to rescue energy conservation from beta spectra that seemed to lose it,
    and the neutrino — so ghostly that a light-year of lead barely thins a beam — was found
    in 1956. Fermi's 1933–34 theory of beta decay is the ancestor of the modern weak
    interaction: the first force described by field exchange in something close to the
    modern sense.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file each emission under its fundamental force" widget="sort-buckets" config={qftEmissionSort} />
  <p>
    Gamma rays carry the first working proof that energy is a field quantum. A photon
    carrying more than 1.022 MeV — twice the electron's rest energy (mc² for an
    electron is 0.511 MeV) — can, in the electric field near a nucleus, convert into
    an electron and a positron: <strong>pair production</strong>, matter created from
    light, exactly the field-theory transaction (a photon of the electromagnetic
    field plucks the electron field and its antiparticle). The reverse process
    completes it: an electron meeting a positron annihilates into photons, rest
    mass returned as energy. PET scanners run on this loop — a positron-emitting
    isotope injected into the patient annihilates where it comes to rest, and the
    back-to-back 511 keV photon pair is triangulated into an image of metabolism.
    Creation and annihilation, the textbook abstractions of QFT, are a routine
    hospital measurement.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> gamma rays prove light is quantised, and pair production turns
    energy into matter and back — creation and annihilation as a hospital measurement.
    Half-life comes next, and it will show quantum randomness itself made measurable.
  </p>

  <h2>Half-life: randomness made measurable</h2>
  <p>
    Half-life (5.2.4) is the syllabus's encounter with genuine quantum randomness: which
    nucleus decays next is unknowable in principle, yet the ensemble decays with perfect
    statistical law — N = N₀e^(−λt), with λ = ln 2 divided by the half-life. After one
    half-life, half remains; after two, a quarter; after ten, about 0.1%. Carbon-14
    (half-life 5,730 years) dates organic remains: a sample retaining 25% of the atmospheric
    proportion is two half-lives old, ≈ 11,460 years; 12.5% gives ≈ 17,190. The decay
    constant works out to λ ≈ 1.21 × 10⁻⁴ per year, meaning a single carbon-14 atom has
    about a one-in-8,000 chance of decaying in any given year (1/λ ≈ 8,270 years is the
    mean lifetime). The QFT point: exponential decay is not a modelling convenience but a
    theorem — an unstable state is a superposition of energy eigenstates, its survival
    amplitude decays exponentially, and the "random click" of a Geiger counter is quantum
    indeterminacy, amplified to macroscopic audibility. Nobody — not Laplace's demon with
    complete knowledge of the present — can say when the next atom goes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="place the marker at the sample's age in half-lives" widget="place" config={qftC14Place} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "half-life means the substance is safe after two
    half-lives." After two half-lives a quarter of the original activity remains — a
    quarter is not zero, and for long-lived isotopes "ten half-lives" (about 1/1,000 left)
    is the usual waste-handling benchmark, with some isotopes needing centuries. Equally
    wrong is the reverse panic: after two half-lives the <em>danger</em> has dropped 75%.
    Exponential decay means "smaller, fast at first, then slower forever" — never a clean
    endpoint.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="find the faulty line in this student's waste-handling note" widget="spot-error" config={qftWasteSpot} />

  <h2>Detection: how the invisible becomes a number</h2>
  <p>
    Detection (5.2.1) is applied quantum measurement. The Geiger–Müller tube: ionising
    radiation kicks electrons off gas atoms inside a tube; a high voltage cascades each
    freed electron into an avalanche, and the pulse is counted — energy-blind but
    dead-reliable. Scintillators trade sensitivity for spectroscopy: the material flashes
    when struck, and the flash's brightness carries the energy. Cloud and bubble chambers
    made tracks visible — condensation or boiling along the ionisation trail — and the
    bubble chamber photographs of the 1950s–60s are how the particle zoo, and then the
    quark model, were charted. Modern detectors (at the LHC, say) layer the logic: tracking
    chambers in a magnetic field (curvature gives momentum), calorimeters (total energy),
    muon detectors (the only particles that get that far) — hundreds of millions of readout
    channels, triggering on interesting events at rates no human could watch. The principle
    every layer shares is unchanged from the school syllabus: radiation is detected by the
    charge it knocks loose, and measurement is avalanche plus statistics.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each detector with its trade-off" widget="match" config={qftDetectorMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> detectors convert invisible quanta into counted avalanches and
    measured energies — measurement as avalanche plus statistics. One piece remains: what
    those numbers obligate, which is the physics behind dose, shielding and ALARA.
  </p>

  <h2>Safety, dose and the physics of interaction</h2>
  <p>
    Safety (5.2.5) is where emission physics meets human biology, and the units do the
    thinking. <em>Activity</em> (becquerels) counts decays per second; <em>absorbed
    dose</em> (grays) counts energy deposited per kilogram; <em>effective dose</em>
    (sieverts) weights by biological damage — a gray of alpha particles inside the body is
    roughly twenty times more damaging than a gray of gamma rays, because alpha's dense
    ionisation track does concentrated local harm. Natural background runs about 2–3
    millisieverts per year worldwide (radon gas — a radioactive daughter of uranium in
    rock — is the largest share, roughly half), a CT scan adds several millisieverts, and
    the annual occupational limit is 20 mSv averaged over five years. The operational rule
    ALARA (as low as reasonably achievable) encodes the honest science: dose-risk is
    roughly linear with no known threshold, so protection is about time, distance and
    shielding — halve your time, double your distance (inverse square), put the right
    absorber between (paper for alpha, aluminium for beta, lead for gamma). Every rule of
    the safety topic is a quantitative consequence of how each emission deposits energy.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the blanks — the three dose units" widget="cloze" config={qftDoseCloze} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "all radiation is equally dangerous, so a granite
    worktop is a Chernobyl." Danger scales with activity, energy, emission type and
    location in the body: a banana (potassium-40, ~0.1 μSv) and a milligray of internal
    alpha are not in the same universe of harm. Conversely "radiation is natural so it's
    harmless" fails the same way — radon in poorly ventilated homes is the leading cause
    of lung cancer among non-smokers. The physics doesn't do adjectives; it does numbers,
    and the numbers are measured per case.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — two doses, two universes of harm" widget="compare" config={qftDoseCompare} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: why are all electrons identical, in the field picture?</li>
    <li>Application: can a 1.5 MeV photon produce an electron–positron pair? What about a 0.8 MeV photon? Justify both with the threshold.</li>
    <li>Why-reasoning: why is exponential decay a theorem of quantum theory rather than a modelling convenience — and why does that make individual decays unpredictable in principle?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Every electron is an excitation of the <em>one</em>
    electron field that fills the universe — two electrons are that field plucked twice. Their
    identity is literal sameness of the underlying field, not a striking coincidence of
    separately manufactured particles. (2) Pair production needs twice the electron's rest
    energy: 2 × 0.511 = 1.022 MeV. A 1.5 MeV photon clears the threshold and converts into a
    pair, the 0.478 MeV surplus becoming kinetic energy (near a nucleus, to conserve momentum);
    a 0.8 MeV photon cannot — no bookkeeping trick creates 1.022 MeV of rest mass from less
    energy. (3) An unstable nucleus is a superposition of energy eigenstates whose survival
    amplitude decays exponentially, so the ensemble law is exact while each individual decay
    stays genuinely indeterminate — the Geiger counter's clicks are quantum randomness
    amplified to audibility, which is why complete knowledge of the present still cannot say
    when the next atom goes.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fields are the ontology, particles are their quanta, diagrams are the method.</li>
    <li>All electrons are identical because they are excitations of one field.</li>
    <li>Alpha, beta and gamma emissions map onto the strong, weak and electromagnetic forces.</li>
    <li>The 1.022 MeV pair-production threshold is rest energy made operational — and the reverse loop runs PET scanners.</li>
    <li>Exponential decay is a theorem of quantum theory; individual decays are unknowable in principle.</li>
    <li>Dose units do the thinking — activity, absorbed dose, effective dose — and ALARA encodes the linear no-threshold science.</li>
    <li>The Standard Model is the most tested theory ever written, and known-incomplete: neutrino mass, dark matter, quantum gravity.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling expert lessons complete the frontier. <em>General relativity</em> treats the
    one force this course could not quantise — gravity as geometry, and the reason a
    quantum theory of it has outlived a century of attacks. <em>Electrodynamics: Maxwell</em>
    is the classical field theory whose quantisation you have just watched; reading it
    before and after this page makes the demotion of particles vivid. For the condensed
    cousin of field thinking, <em>Solid-state physics</em> shows quasiparticles doing
    field-theory work in ordinary matter.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Fields become fundamental.</strong> The Standard Model inventory: six quarks,
    six leptons, the photon, gluon, W and Z bosons, and the Higgs — each a quantum of a
    field that fills space. The deep consequences of the field view: particles are created
    and destroyed (number is not conserved; energy is), identical particles are literally
    identical (one field, many excitations), and forces are exchange processes — the
    electron's repulsion of another electron is a continuous shower of virtual photons.
    Virtual particles are bookkeeping in perturbation theory (internal lines in Feynman
    diagrams, constrained by the energy-time uncertainty budget), but the effects they
    describe are measurable to astonishing precision: QED predicts the electron's magnetic
    moment to about 12 significant figures, the best theory-experiment agreement in
    science. Feynman, Schwinger and Tomonaga took the 1965 Nobel for taming the theory's
    infinities; Wilson's renormalisation group (Nobel 1982) explained <em>why</em> the taming
    works — the infinities encode our ignorance of physics at unexplored short distances,
    absorbed into measured constants.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Higgs and mass.</strong> The Higgs field (1964 mechanism; ripple detected
    2012 at 125 GeV, Nobel 2013) fills space and drags on particles differently by species —
    that drag is what we call rest mass. Without it, electrons would be massless, atoms
    would not bind, and chemistry would not exist; protons and neutrons get almost all
    their mass elsewhere (the strong field's binding energy), which is why "the Higgs gives
    everything mass" is a popular overstatement worth correcting — it gives the
    <em>fundamental particles</em> their masses, and the electron's tiny share is what
    makes matter cool and stable enough to build a syllabus out of.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What the nuclear syllabus cannot see.</strong> The Standard Model is the most
    tested theory ever written — and known-incomplete. Neutrinos oscillate between flavours
    (Nobel 2015), which requires mass the original model forbids. Dark matter demands a
    particle (or a modification of gravity) that no accelerator has produced. Gravity has
    no working quantum field theory — quantising general relativity's graviton naively
    produces uncontrollable infinities, and the problem has outlived a century of attacks.
    The frontier is experimental patience: neutrino observatories (Super-Kamiokande,
    IceCube) watching vats of water for flashes, dark matter detectors a mile underground
    waiting for a recoil that may never come. The syllabus that began with a bouncing alpha
    particle ends, a century later, still asking what most of the universe is made of.
  </p>
  <p>
    The vacuum, in field theory, is not empty: it is the fields' ground state, and its
    fluctuations are measurable. The Casimir effect — two uncharged plates held
    micrometres apart attracting with a tiny but cleanly measured force (1997's
    precision measurement settled decades of doubt) — is the standard demonstration:
    the plates exclude some vacuum modes between them, and the imbalance outside
    pushes them together. Virtual-particle talk is the perturbative description of
    such effects, and the honest expert position is worth stating exactly: the
    <em>calculations</em> (diagrams with internal lines) are unambiguous and match
    experiment; the <em>picture</em> of particles briefly borrowing energy is a
    popularisation, not a claim the theory makes. Fields are the ontology;
    diagrams are the method.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Feynman, QED (1985); Peskin & Schroeder, An Introduction to Quantum Field Theory; ATLAS & CMS, Phys. Lett. B (2012). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
